{
  "requirement": "`Comment` covers source comments, including documentation comments.\n`Annotation` covers language constructs such as attributes and decorators\nthat annotate a declaration. Providers use the common kind when it applies\nand `Other` with a language-defined name for additional kinds.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub enum AttachmentKind {\n    Comment,\n    Annotation,\n    Other(String),\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for AttachmentKind;\nimpl Debug for AttachmentKind;\nimpl PartialEq for AttachmentKind;\nimpl Eq for AttachmentKind;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.outlines.AttachmentKind`. I did not read the implementation.\n\n**Comment**\n- `rust_attachments_keep_the_owner_kind_and_original_bytes` asserts `Comment` for `//!` and `///` doc comments and for a trailing `//` comment.\n- `bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax` asserts `Comment` for block doc comments in Rust, plus comments in Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, YAML, TOML and Markdown.\n- The same test also asserts that comment-like text inside literals, and plain text, is not classified as a comment.\n\n**Annotation**\n- The Rust test asserts `Annotation` for `#[inline]`.\n- The bundled-languages test asserts `Annotation` for Python decorators, including a multi-line decorator on a class.\n- The TypeScript decorator `@dec class C` is asserted not to be an attachment. The spec says `Annotation` covers \"attributes and decorators\", but the bundled TypeScript provider doesn't emit one here. Python decorators are covered, so I did not treat this as a gap.\n\n**Other(String)**\n- `custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot` uses a custom provider that returns `AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\")`. It asserts that the kind survives into the snapshot unchanged and equals `Other(\"marker\".into())`. This covers the language-defined-name case.\n\n**Derived traits**\n- `Clone` is exercised through `a.kind.clone()`.\n- `PartialEq` and `Debug` are exercised through the `assert_eq!` calls that compare kinds.\n- `public_exports_match_the_appendix` in `tests/public_api.rs` covers the declared trait list, including `Eq`. Its `check_declaration` helper compares the requirement's \"Trait implementations\" block with rustdoc's trait-implementation listing. It also compares the declared members and requires `#[non_exhaustive]` where the public-API test enforces it. That test would fail if `Eq`, `Clone`, `Debug` or `PartialEq` were missing or extra.\n\nI found no gap, and there are no acknowledgments to account for.",
